.TH DC 1
.SH NAME
dc \- desk calculator, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dc
[
.B -e
.I script
]
[
.B -f
.I scriptfile
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dc
is a Limbo-native workalike for a bounded subset of
.IR dc (1),
the reverse-Polish desk calculator, compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Dc
reads a program from
.I file
arguments (concatenated in order), or standard input if none are given,
or from
.B -e
.I script
and
.B -f
.I scriptfile
arguments (repeatable, and combinable with each other, evaluated in the
order given before any files). A dc program is a sequence of numbers and
single-character commands operating on a stack.
.PP
A number is pushed by writing it: digits, an optional
.B .
and more digits, with a leading
.B _
for a negative literal (a bare
.B \-
is the subtraction operator, not part of a number). Arithmetic is exact,
arbitrary-precision fixed-point \(em not floating point \(em matching real
.IR dc 's
own arithmetic rather than approximating it.
.SS Commands
.TP
.B + - * /
Pop two values, apply the operator, push the result.
.B /
truncates toward zero to the current scale (see
.BR k ,
below); the others are exact except where noted.
.TP
.B %
Modulo: computes
.I a
\-
.RI ( a / b ,
truncated to the current scale)
.RI * " b" .
.TP
.B ^
Power. The exponent must be an integer (positive or negative); a
negative exponent divides 1 by the positive power, using the current
scale.
.TP
.B v
Square root, truncated to the current scale.
.TP
.B p
Print the top of the stack, followed by a newline, without removing it.
.TP
.B n
Pop the top of the stack and print it, without a trailing newline.
.TP
.B f
Print the whole stack, one value per line, top first, without clearing
it.
.TP
.B c
Clear the stack.
.TP
.B d
Duplicate the top of the stack.
.TP
.B r
Swap the top two values.
.TP
.BI s x
Pop the top of the stack into register
.IR x .
.TP
.BI l x
Push a copy of register
.IR x .
.TP
.BI k
Pop the top of the stack and set it as the scale (number of fraction
digits kept by
.BR / ,
.BR % ,
.BR ^ ,
and
.BR v );
must be a non-negative integer.
.TP
.B #
Comment to end of line.
.TP
.B q
Stop processing the program.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/dc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dctest (1)
.SH BUGS
This is a deliberately bounded subset. No macros
.RI ( [ ... ] ,
.BR x ),
no conditional or looping execution
.RB ( < ,
.BR > ,
.BR = ,
.BR ! ),
no strings, no
.B ?
(read a line as input), no
.B z / Z
(stack depth / digit count), and no
.B i / o
(input/output radix \(em always base 10 in and out).
.PP
Register names are single letters,
.B a
through
.B z
and
.B A
through
.BR Z ,
not real
.IR dc 's
arbitrary single byte; each register holds one value, not real
.IR dc 's
register stack (pushing a second value into an already-occupied register
simply replaces it).
.PP
Precision and rounding were reverse-engineered against the host's own
.I dc
with targeted probes, not read off the POSIX text from memory \(em see
the comment at the top of
.B p9/cmd/dc.b
for exactly what was checked before any of this was written: add and
subtract take the max of the two operands' scales, exactly; multiply's
natural scale is the sum of the operands' scales, truncated toward zero
only if that sum exceeds the larger of the current scale and the two
operands' scales; divide, modulo and square root always truncate to the
current scale; and a value that comes out to exactly zero prints a bare
.B 0
regardless of its scale. All of this is checked byte-for-byte against
.B /usr/bin/dc
on the same program, not asserted from documentation.
